He's affiliated faculty at
the Woods Institute for the environment

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.030
here at Stanford, and he co-directs
the executive program in strategy and

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.340
organization that runs during the summer.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:30.030
Bill studies competition among
organizations and how organizations and

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.200
industries evolve globally.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.630
His prior research includes studies of how
strategic differences and strategic change

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.430
among organizations effect their growth,
performance and survival.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.120
It's now my pleasure to turn
things over to Professor Barnett,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.060
Bill thank you very much for
being here with us today.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.220
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, Dena and
thanks everyone for tuning in.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.350
We'll be talking about innovation and
innovation by design

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:02.420
over the next hour many of
the ideas we talk about.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.830
There is a great deal of research on in.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.290
Rather than having me
go into that research,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:13.530
I’ll often refer to my teaching notes
which I put out on a blog for your

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:19.190
reference should you want to take a look
at it, and here’s the address of that.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.290
So, that way we don't have to go over
things that I've written about at length.

00:01:28.609 --> 00:01:31.365
So, that's where I've put
all my teaching notes and

00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:34.800
you can,
take a look there whenever you want.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.100
And I guess while I'm plugging,
my teaching notes,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.390
we could also talk about
where you can follow me.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.680
On twitter,
which once again is Barnett talks.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.120
So, let's talk about innovation by design

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:58.820
one of the things that we should
make clear first when we talk about

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:04.650
innovation by design is
that as a population

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:11.070
We are not good at predicting
the direction of dramatic change.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:16.580
And that's not just true for,
people generally who maybe aren't experts.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.980
That's especially true for experts.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:23.800
When you look at the big changes that have
come along in systems of innovation over

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.860
the years, all over the world, we have
done a very bad job of predicting them.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.360
So, take for example the world
wide web and the internet.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.170
Today especially,
if you talk to people who are so

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:40.030
young that they've never known the world
without the internet, we can't imagine

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.490
life without the internet I know my kids
often in the middle of a conversation

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.510
will fact check on their smartphone,
in the middle of a conversation.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.970
It's taken for granted that we can have
access to the world's information.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:59.050
But if you go back to 1992,
1993, the very early days,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:04.370
the internet existed, the world wide web
was just being created, in terms of a,

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:09.760
a massive system where Lots of
different computers could go in.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.690
And then take content the way
that we now take for granted.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.110
We have no understanding
of where that was going.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:20.590
It's actually very interesting
that at the time our experts said

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.660
there was no need for the World Wide Web.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:28.490
Including those who had invented
the technologies for the World Wide Web.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:33.960
I remember Bruno De Leon
who was a French software

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.380
developer and
entrepreneur had created a company

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:43.370
based on a software that did
manipulation of photographs.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.659
And back in 1992, 1993.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.960
Bruno was a visitor in my class
on organizational growth,

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:57.500
and Bruno took the stage after we
talked about their technology,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.620
and he talked about the future, and
he said, he envisioned a future.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:06.230
Well lots of people would
go on to computers.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:12.360
Not to use the computer, but to access
photographs from other people's computers.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.270
Now, you hear that today and
you say, but of course.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:15.920
Well I can tell you,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.940
at the end of that class when I looked
at my class ratings, they were fine.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.370
Except for the Billy on session.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.660
And the students were convinced
that this guy was out of his mind,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.370
and that we shouldn't be
having him back in again.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.670
And yet, he was the one person
I remember from that era who

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.520
was actually talking about
the way we use computers today,

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.700
and when you go through the archives
as I have done, you find there

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.870
are very little understanding
of where technologies are going.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.220
So, that's first thing I
think we have to get clear.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.720
We aren't good at predicting innovation,
and

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.640
our experts are particularly not
good at predicting innovation.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.570
But a second point to make when it
comes to understanding innovation

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:04.330
is that we're really good at
retrospectively rationalizing.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.770
Once an innovation comes about,

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:12.110
we quickly realize collectively what
the meaning of an innovation is and

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:16.440
come to assume it, and obviously,
the web is an example of that.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.050
We now take for granted that it exists,
but another great example are these

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:25.060
business school case studies that you
often read about successful companies.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.890
They're often written up in a way that
seems so compelling, that the opportunity

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:35.180
was so clear, often the innovations
are talked about as if they were planned.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.850
I was sitting on
University Avenue in Palo Alto,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.970
enjoying the sunshine
a couple of weeks ago, and

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:49.470
I overheard a couple of people probably
in their 20s talking about Facebook.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.859
Now, I've followed Facebook since
its beginning with case studies.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:58.650
And, most people know about the road
that Facebook has followed to success as

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:04.300
being one that really emerged through lots
of different unexpected developments.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.220
And yet, to hear them talk about Facebook,

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.330
the platform strategy,
the monetization model,

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:16.240
you would think that it had been
planned by a master chess player.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:22.170
And it's that kind of retrospective
rationalization that we're so good at.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.780
In a way, we look at companies often
the way we look at a persons career.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.750
You know, a company's strategy
Is a lot like a person's resume.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:36.720
It is a retrospective rationalization
of that company's life.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.248
Just like a resume is a retrospective
rationalization of your career.

00:06:40.248 --> 00:06:43.880
It's accurate, factually.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.770
Your resume is accurate, but

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.800
there's nobody's life that
really reads like a resume.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:54.720
I often joke that nobody's
resume Says moved to Boston for

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.410
girl, yet it happens all the time.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.480
One of my sons did it.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:03.970
I don't know if he's tuned in today
to the webinar, but ultimately he and

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:08.070
the girl married and so we have evidence
that that was why he moved to Boston.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.990
His resume says something about Harvard.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:15.040
That's the retrospective
rationalization of what happened.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.010
And you can think of your
own resumes that way.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:22.020
Now, I raise this topic of the resume and
rationalization and

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.810
retrospective rationalization of
businesses, not to be cynical, but

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:33.020
to be honest About the fact that
what unfolds makes sense to us only

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:37.770
in retrospect and as humans we are really
good at retrospectively rationalizing.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:42.380
Not only that, it is our job as a leader

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:46.709
to retrospectively rationalize why
our firm wins or why our firm loses.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.860
So I'm not cynical at all about
retrospective rationalization.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:55.887
I just wanna point out that that's
what goes on in organizational life.

00:07:55.887 --> 00:07:58.311
Now when you put these two facts together,

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:01.624
number one that we are not
good at predicting innovation.

00:08:01.624 --> 00:08:05.920
And number two, that we are really
good at retrospectively rationalizing.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.410
It creates a problem.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:14.800
We tend to be very bad at organizing for
innovation.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:19.140
And to explain my point here,
let me tell a story.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:26.280
I remember going to Singapore in the late
90s to lecture on the same topic,

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:27.799
on organizing for innovation.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:32.720
And as many of you may know,
Singapore as a system,

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:38.280
as a city-state, as a society,
is extremely good at planning.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.100
And you only have to go through their
airport to enjoy the fruits of that.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.870
I often wish that many of the other
airports of the world, would

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:49.689
outsource their operations to Singapore,
because Singapore is so good at planning.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:56.860
As I'm there in Singapore, I noticed in
the mall that was by the Weston Hotel,

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:00.650
there was a street musician
playing a guitar, and

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.430
he was very clean had a poncho on.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:05.650
It was air conditioned,
of course, being Singapore.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.790
He was singing Peter, Paul and Mary songs.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.750
Puff the Magic Dragon,
or something like that.

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:16.260
Now, all my education took
place in Berkeley, so

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.730
I know about street musicians.

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.300
On average, they don't sound that good.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.410
They're not very pleasant, they often are
not someone you'd want to hang out with.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.780
These Singapore street musicians
were actually very nice people,

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.600
they looked approachable and in fact,

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:38.700
there was a second one that I saw that
night in the lobby of the Shangri-La.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.360
Now, at that point, I knew that
there was a pattern nothing happens

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.250
in Singapore by accident anyway,
never mind twice.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:51.810
Well, come to find out, that the campaign
that was going on in Singapore that

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:57.870
year was to increase cre to increase
creativity and spontaneity.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:09:59.980
And these street musicians
were part of that campaign.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:02.810
Now think about this.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.860
Let's think about the innovation process.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.680
What's going on in the case
of the Singapore story?

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:13.270
Here's a distribution
of possible outcomes.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.710
And you don't know what your gonna
get in the innovation process.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.660
Some of your outcomes,
in fact, are very unpleasant.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:26.220
So here's my technical symbol for that
some of them are gonna look like genius.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.540
Later, and who knows what
the real distribution looks like.

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:33.790
Here I have most of our innovations
being sort of, in between.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:37.250
And think of musicians, for example.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.850
We want to have a musical
genius come out of a system.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:42.750
We want to come up with
the next Neil Young or

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.728
Bob Dylan or
whoever you see as a musical genius.

00:10:46.728 --> 00:10:51.720
But the system's going to give us lots
of riff raff along the way down here.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.920
Well, Singapore looked at this problem,
they said,

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.400
we would like to have creativity.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:10:59.190
So they managed it.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:00.940
They know how to plan.

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.070
But in that planning, what do they do?

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:09.640
Here's the distribution of
outcomes they produced.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.690
We are not gonna have any of
that foolishness first of all,

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.245
none of those avoidable street
musicians I used to see in Berkeley.

00:11:16.245 --> 00:11:21.460
Uh-oh, the implications of that planning

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:27.100
is that neither will we
have genius over here.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:33.350
In a sense,
the price of genius is foolishness.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.430
When we manage the innovation process,
look what we do.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:40.500
We manage away variance.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.650
The key to innovation is variance.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:48.370
Yet, most of our managerial systems,
right?

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.020
There's our variants.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:55.280
Most of our managerial systems manage for
the mean in here.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:00.700
And this is the problem
with managing innovation.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:04.210
Especially given the way that
we retrospectively rationalize,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:07.155
we think of course,
we can identify innovation.

00:12:07.155 --> 00:12:10.415
But no, we only identify innovation after

00:12:10.415 --> 00:12:15.330
we know the difference between
the fools and the geniuses.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:19.429
And that only happens once we
allow variance to take place.

00:12:19.429 --> 00:12:24.129
It's one of the biggest problems that we
face in the management of innovation,

00:12:24.129 --> 00:12:28.360
is that we tend to manage away
the variance of the system.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:33.840
Therefore, while we get no foolishness,
neither do we get genius.

00:12:36.248 --> 00:12:37.663
So what do we do?

00:12:37.663 --> 00:12:39.891
What are the implications of this?

00:12:39.891 --> 00:12:45.760
Well it turns out,
we can manage to increase variance.

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:48.600
We can think about
increasing the variability

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.650
of what is produced by a system but

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:56.730
it requires a very different philosophical
approach to how we lead organizations.

00:12:56.730 --> 00:13:00.710
And if I had to boil it down Deena,
I would boil it down to this following

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:07.550
statement, our job in leading
innovation is not to know the future.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:14.060
Our job is to create the system
that discovers that future.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:18.585
And that means creating organizations and
larger ecosystems of

00:13:18.585 --> 00:13:23.325
organizations that have high variance
as one of their characteristics.

00:13:23.325 --> 00:13:25.305
That produce lots of foolishness and

00:13:25.305 --> 00:13:29.615
failure as the price we pay to
get those moments of genius.

00:13:31.007 --> 00:13:34.937
Bill, we've had a bunch of questions
already about what you've been saying,

00:13:34.937 --> 00:13:39.367
some about using models to look at
what's happened in the past and try and

00:13:39.367 --> 00:13:41.207
predict what might happen in the future.

00:13:41.207 --> 00:13:46.187
But then you just mentioned having
all these different ways of,

00:13:46.187 --> 00:13:49.972
of innovating, I guess, doing different
work, so how do you, how do you do that?

00:13:49.972 --> 00:13:52.840
&gt;&gt; Hm-mm.
&gt;&gt; How do you take an organization that

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:57.760
typically develops one or two products and
allow for that type of innovation or

00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:59.110
innovative work?

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.640
&gt;&gt; Yes, now of course, there's a
tremendous amount of work on this, Deena,

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:05.920
with respect to the,
in managing the innovation process.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.170
And I think if I had to boil it down,

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:12.020
especially when we're talking
about predictive models.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:16.950
Remember every model predicts
a distribution of outcomes.

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:22.800
It, it has an on average prediction, but
it also has, if you will, a variance.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:29.290
And so, in a way, our best models,
the ones that are most accurate,

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:34.200
are the ones that are,
in many ways, minimizing variance.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:38.889
So ironically, while they'll do a very
good job of predicting those things in

00:14:38.889 --> 00:14:43.732
the future that should follow from what
we've already experienced in the past.

00:14:43.732 --> 00:14:45.832
Precisely for that reason,

00:14:45.832 --> 00:14:51.040
they'll do a very bad job of
predicting completely new realities.

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:52.089
Let me give you an example.

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:56.530
I studied the evolution of
the computer industry in a,

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:58.680
in a book that I published
a few years ago.

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:00.760
I'm not plugging the book.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:04.830
In fact, my wife describes it as a book
that was written not to be read.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:07.430
So I'll sum up everything you
would ever wanna know out of that

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:08.700
book in the next minute.

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.869
And so don't bother with reading it.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:16.230
But in looking at the history
of the computer industry.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:23.930
The early firms, those in
the mainframe era, were tremendous

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:28.550
at modeling changes as they were taking
place in terms of the mainframe computer.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.260
It wasn't just IBM,
there were a number of firms involved.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:37.740
Tremendous amount of technological
expertise took us from the very

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:43.180
early computers through to computers that
were used in large scale industry and

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:45.650
government and military and so forth.

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:53.425
Now famously, and probably most of you
know this story, computing changed.

00:15:53.425 --> 00:15:57.407
Coming into the 1960s,
moving into the 1970s and, and

00:15:57.407 --> 00:16:00.505
into the 1980s, during During that period,

00:16:00.505 --> 00:16:05.410
there was a shift towards what we now
talk about as distributed computing.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:09.250
This is prior to the personal computer,
prior to the microcomputer.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:12.060
A shift in the logic of computing.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:17.430
If you do want to read about it actually,
the novel Soul of the New Machine is a,

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.360
is a great reference for that.

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:26.200
And many of the companies that were
starting up then began to use a new term.

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:28.680
The term was hacking and hacker.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:32.000
There was a hacker code of of ethics.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.460
That said computing should
be accessible to all.

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:37.430
That there should be a much more
distributed bottom up approach.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:42.740
Now albeit the fact that most of
the people involved in that movement have

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:46.280
the largess of Stanford and
MIT at their disposal.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:48.440
So, it wasn't quite a populist movement.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:53.330
But nonetheless, the idea was that you
could access computers from a distributed

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:57.040
system, rather than through
a centralized mainframe system.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:01.540
Well, it turns out that that logic,
everything about that logic of organizing,

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:06.370
competing, designing computers was so
different than anything that you could

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:10.580
imagine in the mainframe era, that all
those models of the development of

00:17:10.580 --> 00:17:17.290
the mainframe computer, that emphasize
scale and economies of scale.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:22.480
We're in a sense disrupted by this
movement to distributed computing.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:27.000
The better you did at developing
the mainframe, the more inappropriate

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.880
was everything about what you did for
distributed computing.

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:31.640
And therefore,

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:35.870
the mainframe firms that tried to move
into distributed computing failed.

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:37.940
Now here's what's really
ironic about this.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:43.980
Once the distributed computing firms
came to blossom the digital equipment

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:51.260
corporations data general, many of
these much storied innovative firms.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:57.770
They were talked about then as the
obviously novel disruptive organizations.

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:02.110
Well, then along comes the microcomputer.

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:08.580
With the microprocessor, the microcomputer
is yet again another logic of computing

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.815
that was completely different
from the design characteristics

00:18:12.815 --> 00:18:16.635
of distributed computing systems required
a different kind of organization,

00:18:16.635 --> 00:18:21.785
much more focus on distribution, much more
of an inclusion of the general public and

00:18:21.785 --> 00:18:24.115
retail markets, and so forth.

00:18:24.115 --> 00:18:26.515
Completely different
approach to software design.

00:18:28.135 --> 00:18:34.180
As late as the early 1980s,
in the Stanford library,

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:38.780
there still are compu- books on
the computer industry that dismissed

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:45.110
the microcomputer as a toy used for
trivial computing purposes.

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:47.270
Once again, a disruption had awry, had,

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:51.460
had come along, where our old logic
didn't apply to the new logic.

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:55.200
The old models weren't good at
predicting the new development.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:59.320
The new developments were taking the world
in a completely different direction.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:04.160
Now look, I bring up this example
not because it's exceptional.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.210
These kinds of changes, these completely

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:13.440
unexpected transformations of
industry through innovation

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:17.470
happen across all industries, and
are happening at an increasing rate.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:23.890
My point is that the models that predict,

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:28.560
because they're based on history,
are particularly bad at under,

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:33.320
at, at at, at predicting how these
innovations will take place.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:37.820
The economy, and different economies
are better or worse at this, but

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:40.870
the economy allows for lots of variance.

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:43.540
And so
the high variance parts of the economy

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:45.980
are the parts of the economy that
come up with these innovations.

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:52.780
You know, many people come to Silicon
Valley in order to understand innovation.

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:54.070
And they will come from other countries.

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:55.880
They'll say we'd love to
have a Silicon Valley.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:20:00.550
And the very first thing I say is well,
let's look at your failure rate.

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:02.960
And you often get surprised.

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:05.228
They say, "No we actually,
we prefer not to have failure.

00:20:05.228 --> 00:20:07.236
We, we want to have innovation.

00:20:07.236 --> 00:20:08.860
And I say, but of course.

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:12.810
But the best predictor of innovation,
which is genius, is whether or

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:16.200
not this system is allowing for failure.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.846
Because that tells me it's
a high variance system.

00:20:18.846 --> 00:20:23.520
And it's a high-variance system that
produces both genius and folly, and

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:27.670
typically upends the models that
were predictive in the past.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:30.660
&gt;&gt; This is amazing.

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:32.220
We've had a lot of questions about that.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:35.636
And first, someone was asking about,

00:20:35.636 --> 00:20:39.620
you, can't you just respond quickly
to other changes that are going on?

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:42.160
But then when you look
at the technologies it,

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:43.800
it doesn't seem like you
could really keep up.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:47.730
So it seems like you need to have
a large group of people working on lots

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:51.310
of different projects and so
can you talk a little bit about how you,

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:52.360
how do you do that?

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:55.040
How do you make it safe for
people to fail in a company

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:57.850
knowing that you have no idea what's
gonna happen in the future and

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:01.890
that one of those things might be
the new direction of your company?

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:03.980
&gt;&gt; That's a good a good question.

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:07.490
DNN it's, it's typically the one
that comes up at this point.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:09.750
Once again there is a lot of work on this.

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:15.680
I guess to sum it up, I would say that
what matters most is knowing whether or

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:20.340
not you're trying to capitalize on
existing technologies, or whether or

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:25.670
not you're trying to move in directions
that are likely to be disruptive.

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:29.210
Because look, when we're, when we're
trying to capitalize on existing

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:35.220
technologies, we,
we need to focus on what is already known,

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:40.340
and plan for
time to market given those technologies.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:43.400
Now let's talk about Seagate
as a great example of this.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:48.137
When Steve Luczo initially took
over as CEO of, of Seagate, and

00:21:48.137 --> 00:21:54.628
now I'm going way back to the late 90s
right around the turn of the the century

00:21:54.628 --> 00:22:00.796
that company was a technological leader
that had been perennially unprofitable.

00:22:00.796 --> 00:22:02.476
And when you look at why,

00:22:02.476 --> 00:22:07.456
it had to do with the fact that it was
constantly lagging in time to market.

00:22:07.456 --> 00:22:12.963
Well Steve shifted Seagate at that
time to a time to market strategy.

00:22:12.963 --> 00:22:18.328
And the way he did that was to remind the
organization and really move the culture,

00:22:18.328 --> 00:22:22.997
and structure and routines of
the organization to emphasize using only

00:22:22.997 --> 00:22:29.390
existing technologies, so that they could
make customer defined time milestones.

00:22:29.390 --> 00:22:32.600
Right, so if you're gonna come out and of
course we're going back in time now, but

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:36.900
you know you're coming out with
say 20 megabyte disk drive that

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:41.370
say a consumer a products
company like a Dell.

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:47.430
Or maybe, a disk array firm like
an EMC needs by a certain date,

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:49.000
then you gotta make that date.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:52.360
And if you're gonna miss that date and not
have those technological specifications

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:56.890
because you were trying to push the edge
of technology, that's unacceptable.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:23:01.290
So interestingly, Seagate became much
more responsive on time to market.

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:04.880
But it did so
by focusing on existing technologies.

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:12.380
Meanwhile, simultaneously, in a separate
part of the organization, Steve Luczo and

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:17.550
his team put together Seagate research
which was based back, it's a long story, I

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:22.580
won't tell you all the details and I write
it about in my teaching notes on my blog.

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:23.990
But it was based elsewhere,

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:28.450
it was separate from much of
the organization's production processes.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:34.380
And that part of the organization
did a lot more blue sky research.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:37.230
In the fundamentals of material science so

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:41.730
that they were ready to begin to
incorporate solid state memory and

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:47.110
other kinds of memory which we now
see combined with what you'd think

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:51.440
of as traditional disk drives in the much
more hybrid products that you see today.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:54.349
So there were both of
these efforts going on.

00:23:54.349 --> 00:23:59.397
Here at Stanford,
our professor Charles O'Reilly has done

00:23:59.397 --> 00:24:04.534
a great deal of research on,
This mixing of the focus on exploiting

00:24:04.534 --> 00:24:09.420
what we know and the organizing
to explore what we don't know.

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:14.390
But I have to say that even though
there has been a great deal of work,

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:15.990
especially by professor O'Reilly and

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:20.739
his colleagues on this difficult problem,
it is a problem.

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:24.480
Because after all,
let's go back to that distribution.

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.550
Okay.
So if this is this distribution.

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.280
Who knows what it's actually shaped like.

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:32.270
Perhaps it's skewed differently.

00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:35.440
And down here we have these ideas
that aren't going to work out and

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:37.690
up here we have these ideas
that are going to work out.

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:43.950
In the economy when these
innovations come along,

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:48.630
the economy draws a selection threshold,
okay?

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:53.680
And these firms, these efforts,
these innovations are gonna be deselected.

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:56.640
And the evolutionary process of
the economy leaves us with these

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:57.910
successful firms.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:03.100
You have to ask yourself, where did
the costs of failure go in the economy?

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:09.200
And the answer in capitalism is that
those costs are what the finance

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:14.610
markets in a sense,
distribute across risk takers,

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:18.370
according to the level of risk
that is seen in these projects.

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:23.840
So, capitalism is actually
reasonably good at allowing for

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:26.690
this kind of an experimentation process.

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:30.760
When you try to explore for
new possibilities inside companies,

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:35.650
in a sense you bring inside
the company these costs.

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.542
Which means wide open blue sky
innovation could be cost prohibitive.

00:25:40.542 --> 00:25:44.430
I mean to put it another way if you're
gonna, if you really want to win

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:48.780
the lottery you'll by all the lottery
tickets but that's not a, as

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:53.625
business school professors would put it,
positive net present value proposition.

00:25:53.625 --> 00:25:58.140
&gt;&gt; Bill, we have a bunch of
questions on that as well and

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:01.860
so if, if you have your research area or

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:06.630
even a group of people who can fail trying
to create that sense that you can fail.

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:08.170
How do you reward the failure?

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:08.810
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:09.720
Yeah.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.170
Well you know that's a,
it's a tough thing.

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:15.420
And I think one of the ways to look at
the problem is to break the different

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:19.500
techniques of organizing and
leadership into the different parts.

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:22.040
Some are easier to deal with than others.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.120
&gt;&gt; So
let's look at the easiest to deal with and

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:28.380
that would be the sort of architecture and
routines of your organization.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:33.100
Reporting relationships,
structures, incentives.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:37.320
Those you can typically manipulate
reasonably easily compared to say,

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:40.800
changing the kind of people
that work in an organization or

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:44.610
changing the culture of an organization,
those are harder to deal with.

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:47.130
So we look at the easier
to deal with techniques,

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:50.390
the organization's structure,
its routines.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:54.570
There what we see is that
the most innovative organizations

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:59.360
are on the one hand,
structured in a way that they separate

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:03.920
out those who must make the numbers
on a quarter to quarter basis,

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:09.630
from those who are exploring for
new possibilities.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:11.500
And it's, once again, Charles O'Reilly,

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:15.630
professor Charles O'Reilley's work that
has looked a lot at this separation of

00:27:15.630 --> 00:27:20.869
the different parts of what he terms
the ambidextrous organization.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:25.480
But also then if you look at the different
routines and so for example,

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:30.000
incentive systems which were
implied by your question Deena

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:34.420
there, there, the jury's out.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:36.750
Incentive systems can
become too complicated.

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:39.930
If I had to boil down what I've
seen in the research so far,

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:42.520
I would say it's not exactly determinate,
but

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:47.580
there is some evidence that incentive
systems that work on behavior rather than

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:52.720
outcome are more forgiving with
respect to failure, right.

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:57.850
So think this through if someone
is your direct report and

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:01.380
they've done what in your
book are the right things

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:05.570
with respect to a process
of innovation but

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:11.170
it hasn't worked out you can reward
them for having gone through the process

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:16.720
of innovating even though in that
particular trial it hasn't worked out.

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:19.589
And we can take a great lesson here
from the world of entrepreneurs.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:26.040
The big movement now in entrepreneurship
following the work of Eric Reese and

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:30.190
Steve Blank really takes
us back to the old theories

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:32.750
of the great professor James March.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:36.300
Many of the ideas that I'm talking
about on innovation came out of

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:37.970
James March's work.

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:42.730
And if you had to boil it down this
approach says as follows, and it's written

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:47.980
at, at length by Reese Anblank,
as well as James, March and his students.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:54.450
If you had to boil it down,
the innovation process comes down to this.

00:28:54.450 --> 00:29:00.590
We try something, we find out why it
doesn't work, and then we update.

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:03.390
And you know, for those of you who
studied the scientific method in school,

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:08.770
you know you have your theory which we
are going to call a strategy, and then

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:13.540
you take that, and you test it empirically
as a scientist would in the sciences.

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:18.460
We're going to call that the market or the
customer and than based on what goes wrong

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:23.800
here with the customer,
we update the strategy.

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:27.710
Now if we spend a lot of time
up here in a planning process,

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:32.350
planning the strategy,
we better hope to be right.

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:35.860
Because we put a lot of money into it and
sunk a lot of time.

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:41.550
If instead we minimize how much time we
spend planning and as quickly as possible,

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:45.980
go to the customer,
we learn why we're wrong more quickly and

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:50.870
go through this iterative process of
learning Which as many of our viewers know

00:29:50.870 --> 00:29:55.740
has become very popular in Silicon Valley
and leading the startup movement.

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:59.070
And in fact many large
organizations are using

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:02.790
when they engage in Agile
development processes and

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:07.080
other such processes that rely
on iterative improvement.

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:07.730
Now remember,

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:12.520
the secret to iterative improvement
is not getting it right initially.

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:15.260
It's getting a process
of learning in place, so

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:20.190
that we converge over time on a better
solution through trial and error.

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:25.080
Now, there are a lot of problems with
this, and so I'm not saying this is

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:29.180
a panacea, I remember being asked to
speak on this topic to a group of airline

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:34.390
executives in a restaurant that was
overlooking San Francisco Airport.

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:40.600
Which would have been fine, except that
down on the runway was the burned out hull

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:46.760
of the airliner that tragically had,

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:51.720
had a crash landing at San Francisco
Airport here, about a year ago.

00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:57.050
And it was very difficult for
me with a straight face to talk about the,

00:30:57.050 --> 00:31:01.120
importance of failure and
the need to fail to a bunch of people

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:05.300
in the aeronautics industry overlooking
the burned out hull of an aircraft.

00:31:05.300 --> 00:31:10.980
I don't want a lot of foolishness if
someone is running a nuclear powerplant.

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:13.920
There are lots of times we
don't want to see failure.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:17.160
In fact, if I want a nuclear powerplant,
Ron, I probably want to put Singapore on

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:20.770
the job because we're not
gonna see any foolishness.

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:25.910
So you have to do this kind of approach
to innovation in an appropriate way.

00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:31.600
And it may be that a lot of prototyping
and developing goes on behind the scenes.

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:38.300
Before a product is declared ready for
launching as, as a final product.

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:43.460
If you look at the American changes
in the American healthcare system.

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:48.650
It wasn't surprising to a lot of us here
in the Silicon Valley that the initial

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:54.940
rollout of the software to sign up people
for healthcare had a lot of problems.

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:57.310
That's often how software is developed.

00:31:57.310 --> 00:32:02.126
It was probably, it was clearly
inappropriate to run the experiment in

00:32:02.126 --> 00:32:07.263
real time on the American people While
they were supposed to be signing up for

00:32:07.263 --> 00:32:08.242
healthcare.

00:32:08.242 --> 00:32:12.662
You really should have gone
through that iterative process of

00:32:12.662 --> 00:32:16.657
development in prototypes on
test markets in advance so

00:32:16.657 --> 00:32:21.430
that they roll out ends up
being a a much better product.

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:27.070
And yet, when you look at those who
are now retrospectively rationalizing

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:30.830
the flawed rollout of
the healthcare software.

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:34.245
There's a lot of talk about,
couldn't it have been planned better?

00:32:34.245 --> 00:32:37.740
No, this wasn't an issue of planning.

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:39.480
It's that the development process for

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:43.730
something really different,
requires lots of iterative learning, and

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:46.600
that wasn't built into that
development process in advance.

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:52.470
&gt;&gt; So, we've had a few questions
on how to finance innovation.

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:53.860
So how much money, is,

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:58.450
is there any research that tells a company
how much they should be spending on people

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:01.228
who aren't really creating anything
that's gonna come to market quickly?

00:33:01.228 --> 00:33:03.647
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:33:03.647 --> 00:33:08.750
Well, it, it turns out

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:15.280
that it is very difficult for
firms to imitate venture capitalists.

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:17.850
And so, I don't know if the question
really wanted to go at that.

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:19.320
Let me go at that issue though.

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:25.720
So the venture capital community
is a very different approach

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:30.660
to finance than what we see
in many other communities.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:37.555
One of the prominent venture capital
firms here in Silicon Valley,

00:33:37.555 --> 00:33:41.005
for example, had a bit of
a crisis a couple of years ago.

00:33:41.005 --> 00:33:45.145
Some of my colleagues were
among their leadership and

00:33:45.145 --> 00:33:48.685
I was very interested to hear
how they dealt with the crisis.

00:33:48.685 --> 00:33:50.635
They had found they had a real problem

00:33:51.865 --> 00:33:54.925
with the failure rate of
their portfolio companies.

00:33:57.550 --> 00:34:00.770
The problem was that their
failure rate was going down.

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:07.370
They were becoming concerned
that their access to deal flow,

00:34:07.370 --> 00:34:11.830
their view of what was going
on in terms of new venture

00:34:11.830 --> 00:34:15.390
creation was perhaps
becoming too conservative.

00:34:15.390 --> 00:34:18.220
They were getting not enough foolishness.

00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:21.010
Now, that in itself wouldn't
be a problem except that it

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:23.580
might imply they're not
getting enough genius.

00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:27.450
And so once again, going back to that
model where you wanna increase variance,

00:34:27.450 --> 00:34:32.505
the perspective, the venture community
is of the most successful firms,

00:34:32.505 --> 00:34:34.455
is to magnify that variance.

00:34:35.515 --> 00:34:37.565
And in, in,

00:34:37.565 --> 00:34:41.165
not a, not because you're trying to
make money out of 90% of your ventures.

00:34:41.165 --> 00:34:43.745
No, you're only going to be making
money out of a very small proportion of

00:34:43.745 --> 00:34:44.259
your ventures.

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:50.820
Now that model works well for
firms that specialize in it and

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:56.160
come to understand the logic of
investing for variance sake.

00:34:56.160 --> 00:35:00.530
It does not work so well, the empirical
record shows, for corporations.

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:05.470
For businesses, it's often very difficult
for the internal corporate venturing arm

00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:10.430
of business, to in a sense, imitate what
goes on in the venture capital community.

00:35:11.456 --> 00:35:15.660
And that empirical fact,

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:20.230
itself, should cause people inside
large corporations to think twice

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:22.780
before they try to play the game
of the venture capitalist.

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:27.280
I think it's much more important for
businesses to have strategic direction,

00:35:27.280 --> 00:35:30.700
to understand why they
go to work everyday.

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:34.820
And to try to make good on the investments
that are already in place.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:39.960
And let me return again to the example
I gave you earlier of Steve Luczo

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:41.415
at Seagate.

00:35:41.415 --> 00:35:48.580
For years, ever since solid
state flash memory came along,

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:52.150
people have been worrying about what
would go on with the Winchester and

00:35:52.150 --> 00:35:53.390
the disk drive and so forth.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:59.610
And, and yet, during that period
of great concern, Steve and

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:03.580
his team managed to
continue to make money, and

00:36:03.580 --> 00:36:06.599
there have been ups and downs in that
business, as there are in any business.

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:12.450
But they, could not take their eye off
the ball, that they were supplying large

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:17.230
numbers of storage devices to
companies all over the world.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:22.600
And that is a very different
strategic task than experimenting

00:36:22.600 --> 00:36:24.180
in going in new directions.

00:36:24.180 --> 00:36:26.260
Do they need to have that
kind of experimentation?

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:29.780
Absolutely, but that doesn't
make them venture capitalists.

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:33.000
The venture capital business
is a very different business.

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:38.933
And I would caution firms that try to
simply imitate the venture capital model.

00:36:38.933 --> 00:36:42.915
&gt;&gt; So, going back to
the rewarding failure, you know,

00:36:42.915 --> 00:36:47.261
how, how long do you let someone
go on a path of something?

00:36:47.261 --> 00:36:50.442
And, and had, but well,
I'll just stop with that.

00:36:50.442 --> 00:36:52.523
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; How long, how long do you let it go?

00:36:52.523 --> 00:36:56.102
And then how do you communicate that
to the rest of the company to, so that

00:36:56.102 --> 00:37:00.283
the people in the company who are working
on something that's failing all the time.

00:37:00.283 --> 00:37:04.540
Aren't seen as lesser than the people
who are creating the products?

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:05.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.
You know, Deena,

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.820
this is this is the big question.

00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:09.210
This is the million dollar question,
right?

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:15.930
So, let's, let's take a look at
the way that great change comes about.

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:19.630
If this is the performance
of an organization, and

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:22.850
we know these performance
changes over time, right, so

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:24.800
we're gonna have performance
changing over time.

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:32.560
When we're here, in terms of our
performance, if, if we, let's say,

00:37:32.560 --> 00:37:36.780
if we stay the course, we would just keep
performance as is, just hypothetically.

00:37:38.310 --> 00:37:41.430
But if we move in a new strategic
direction, there's going to be risk,

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:42.890
of course.

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:46.440
But we have a vision of getting to
a much higher level of performance,

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:50.790
by making a strategic change.

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:54.450
The trouble is,
we have to get from here to here.

00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:57.280
Right?
We have to somehow get, get up there.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:00.980
And, and the, the,
the execution of this is not immediate.

00:38:00.980 --> 00:38:04.870
Which, Deena, in your question,
is the underlying issue.

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:07.870
Here's what actually happens when
an innovation is successful.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:12.180
When an inno,
when a big innovation is successful,

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:15.450
it actually makes performance worse,
initially.

00:38:18.476 --> 00:38:23.071
Until at some point, and this varies by
context and the nature of the innovation,

00:38:23.071 --> 00:38:25.010
we end up improving performance.

00:38:25.010 --> 00:38:27.940
And of course,
the exact shape of this curve can vary.

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:32.022
Some people talk about it as the so
called J curve.

00:38:32.022 --> 00:38:36.118
It, now this is a problem,
because if we're down here and

00:38:36.118 --> 00:38:41.989
you're working at an organization down
there and things have been getting worse.

00:38:41.989 --> 00:38:46.313
You're looking at leadership saying,
I'm not sure what you folks are doing, but

00:38:46.313 --> 00:38:48.923
things are worse today
than they were yesterday.

00:38:48.923 --> 00:38:52.643
And then if leadership stays the course,
they say yeah, but now look,

00:38:52.643 --> 00:38:55.498
things are even worse today
than they were yesterday.

00:38:55.498 --> 00:39:00.519
And when we talk about leadership,
often, leadership in

00:39:00.519 --> 00:39:07.286
a process of innovation has to make clear
the logic of what must be true up here.

00:39:07.286 --> 00:39:11.971
Which helps your people understand why
it is that we're having to endure this

00:39:11.971 --> 00:39:15.710
difficult process of change
in order to get there.

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:18.280
That's why strategy is important.

00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:24.970
Getting back to that iterative learning
process I was describing earlier,

00:39:24.970 --> 00:39:27.740
things might not get better as we iterate.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:34.277
We might be learning about changes we have
to make that are especially difficult.

00:39:34.277 --> 00:39:38.772
Lots of end to end solutions that
require the whole system to shift

00:39:38.772 --> 00:39:40.252
are very difficult.

00:39:40.252 --> 00:39:46.300
I remember when years ago the folks at
Qualcomm were talking about a future,

00:39:46.300 --> 00:39:52.180
or when Jacobs, Paul Jacobs and
their team were talking about a future.

00:39:52.180 --> 00:39:57.687
Where the wireless system would
move large amounts of data.

00:39:57.687 --> 00:40:02.019
Now today, of course, we take that for
granted in the retrospective

00:40:02.019 --> 00:40:06.876
rationalization process that I was talking
about earlier But at the time that

00:40:06.876 --> 00:40:11.957
Irwin Jacobs, and Paul Jacobs and that
team at Qualcomm were first talking about

00:40:11.957 --> 00:40:17.870
that future, there was a tremendous amount
of debate about whether that was true.

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:24.040
In fact, in testifying before the European
regulators, on the subject of whether

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:29.420
the standard that Qualcomm was developing,
so-called CDMA technology, back then.

00:40:29.420 --> 00:40:34.370
And, of course, there's been a lot of
technical change since then, but, but

00:40:34.370 --> 00:40:38.730
in that debate which is now
a couple of decades ago,

00:40:40.050 --> 00:40:44.810
the, European regulators
in fact chose to not allow

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:49.420
CDMA technology into the European system,

00:40:49.420 --> 00:40:52.230
because they were unconvinced that
it was going to be effective.

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:57.990
There had been professors here at
Stanford who had engineering professors,

00:40:57.990 --> 00:41:03.090
who had published that the ideas
behind Irwin Jacobs' innovation

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:08.220
violated the laws of psychics, so
a tremendous amount of disagreement.

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:12.900
And yet, the folks in the Qualcomm team
remained convinced that although it was

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:17.790
difficult at first, that ultimately
if they could get a system in

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:23.290
place that allowed for
the efficient digitization of data,

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:27.610
that we would be able to do
a tremendous amount of movement of,

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:33.180
of that data among small handheld devices,
which of course, turned out to be true.

00:41:33.180 --> 00:41:35.760
But it was much debated for years.

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:40.280
When those systems were, were really
those of you who lived through that

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:45.160
as consumers remember it was hard to get a
phone call, never mind pull up a website,

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:48.350
or make reservations,
all the kinds of things we do today.

00:41:48.350 --> 00:41:53.060
Now, we're closer to having
fulfilled that vision, but

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:57.670
getting from here to there
is a very difficult road.

00:41:57.670 --> 00:42:03.590
It requires a belief by people in
organizations in their strategy.

00:42:03.590 --> 00:42:08.140
It requires leadership that
aims at that end state.

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:12.700
And what's interesting is it doesn't mean
that the leaders know all the details

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:15.210
about what will have to
unfold to get there.

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:17.165
But they know what the end state is, and

00:42:17.165 --> 00:42:20.315
they help their people understand
the logic of that business.

00:42:23.513 --> 00:42:27.627
&gt;&gt; Wow, I'm just thinking about that
a little bit because there are,

00:42:27.627 --> 00:42:31.739
how to communicate that to everyone and
how to be a strong leader is,

00:42:31.739 --> 00:42:33.445
sounds very challenging.

00:42:33.445 --> 00:42:34.140
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:38.490
&gt;&gt; Can you talk a little bit about how to,
how you might help the organization

00:42:38.490 --> 00:42:42.410
move towards,
I guess having that sort of strategy, and

00:42:42.410 --> 00:42:45.825
having that sort of leadership
that can really stay the course?

00:42:45.825 --> 00:42:46.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good question.

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:50.810
I mean, we've seen it happen in
the form of strong leaders, as you say,

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:53.960
Dina, and
obviously Steve Jobs comes to mind.

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:59.840
When I give leadership lectures
around the world I often

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:05.100
ask the audiences that I face the question
who, what is an example of a great leader?

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:12.290
And one of the things I find so amusing
is that I'll often have military leaders,

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:16.002
religious leaders brought up,
depending on context.

00:43:16.002 --> 00:43:20.143
I've had audiences name as their great,

00:43:20.143 --> 00:43:25.463
as examples of great leaders Genghis Khan,

00:43:25.463 --> 00:43:29.839
Jesus Christ, the prophet Mohammed,

00:43:29.839 --> 00:43:34.334
Mahatma Gandhi, Napoleon.

00:43:34.334 --> 00:43:37.585
Vladimir Putin was brought up recently.

00:43:37.585 --> 00:43:39.540
You can guess where I was.

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:45.280
And, but in almost every situation,
people named Steve Jobs.

00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:52.000
And clearly he's seen as the icon for
this kind of visionary leadership.

00:43:52.000 --> 00:43:55.080
People used to say that
Steve was surrounded

00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:57.910
by a so-called reality distortion field.

00:43:57.910 --> 00:44:01.980
Now the point there is,
if Steve believed something was possible,

00:44:01.980 --> 00:44:05.290
it often helped others to come to
believe that that was possible.

00:44:06.320 --> 00:44:07.940
But here's the problem.

00:44:07.940 --> 00:44:13.340
Prior to Steve's fantastic successes
in recent years, before his

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:19.980
tragic and early death, prior to that,
if you go back into the 1990s,

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:27.530
Steve Jobs was often looked at as
an example of bad leadership style.

00:44:27.530 --> 00:44:29.930
And it's hard for
us to even imagine that now, but

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:35.570
there were case studies about Steve using
a very dogmatic approach to leadership and

00:44:35.570 --> 00:44:38.652
firing people when maybe
that was inappropriate.

00:44:38.652 --> 00:44:43.121
Steve's own board unseated him, as people
may know if they've read about the,

00:44:43.121 --> 00:44:44.381
the Steve Jobs story.

00:44:44.381 --> 00:44:47.943
The, the jury was out, so
if we go back to our J curve, and

00:44:47.943 --> 00:44:53.370
if we go back to our J curve, there were
times for Steve when he was down here.

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:56.660
And at that point,
a lot of people had doubts about him.

00:44:56.660 --> 00:45:01.300
It's also true that it
wasn't clear that Steve knew

00:45:01.300 --> 00:45:05.350
what Apple was going to look
like once it came up here.

00:45:05.350 --> 00:45:09.680
What he did do is he put in place
a system that could discover it.

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:12.860
And so, you know,

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:16.920
I think you wanna think about that
in terms of what we do as leaders.

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:17.780
You know, as leaders,

00:45:17.780 --> 00:45:22.430
it may be that we don't actually know the
direction the organization needs to go in.

00:45:22.430 --> 00:45:26.060
But instead, we want to create
a system that can discover it.

00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:27.206
One of the ways I like to,

00:45:27.206 --> 00:45:31.260
to help people think about this is to run
through the following thought experiment.

00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:33.500
Ask yourself this question.

00:45:33.500 --> 00:45:37.980
When you have a good idea, does everybody
have to agree with that idea for

00:45:37.980 --> 00:45:38.770
it to be correct?

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:42.430
And most people, if I ask them
that question, will say, well, no.

00:45:43.630 --> 00:45:47.286
I'm my own person, you know,
I could come up with an idea and

00:45:47.286 --> 00:45:50.821
even if people don't agree with it,
it might be correct.

00:45:50.821 --> 00:45:57.350
And yet, when we have an idea, ask
yourself, what's the first thing we do?

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:01.280
We ask people.

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:03.260
We turn to other people, we say,
what do you think of this?

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:08.580
And if the people we ask don't
like the idea, what do we do then?

00:46:10.550 --> 00:46:12.130
We often ask someone else.

00:46:14.770 --> 00:46:16.200
So what is it we're doing?

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:19.750
On the one hand, we say we don't need to
have everybody agree with us in order for

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:20.710
an idea to be good.

00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:24.500
On the other hand, we tend to really
care about what other people think.

00:46:25.950 --> 00:46:29.423
Well, here's what I
think's going on there, so

00:46:29.423 --> 00:46:32.164
just bear with me as I run through this.

00:46:32.164 --> 00:46:33.470
Okay, look.

00:46:33.470 --> 00:46:36.520
When we have an idea that's innovative,
we might be right.

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:41.630
But, of course, we might be wrong, and

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:46.760
the more interesting the idea, the less
we can know in advance which is which.

00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:49.660
Or to put it another way,
if we know in advance,

00:46:49.660 --> 00:46:53.520
we have enough information to know
the difference between genius and

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:57.950
foolishness, the idea
isn't that innovative.

00:46:59.700 --> 00:47:04.020
But what we can detect right away is
whether or not people agree with us.

00:47:05.040 --> 00:47:07.540
If we ask other people and
they're all agreeing with us,

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:09.700
we've got ourselves a consensus idea.

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:15.250
If we ask other people and
there's a lot of debate and a lot of

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:20.002
the people that we ask do not agree,
let's call that a non-consensus idea.

00:47:24.088 --> 00:47:26.640
Okay, so look at the situation we're in.

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:28.380
What we can observe?

00:47:28.380 --> 00:47:32.450
We can observe whether people agree,
or whether they, they don't agree.

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:34.202
What do we wish we could observe?

00:47:34.202 --> 00:47:37.789
We wish we could observe whether
we're right or whether we're wrong,

00:47:37.789 --> 00:47:38.820
and we can't tell.

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:45.460
Now, for sake of discussion,
let's imagine we're right.

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:51.379
Where would you rather be
if you knew you were right?

00:47:51.379 --> 00:47:56.743
Well, if you think that through,
look back at that Irwin Jacobs story.

00:47:56.743 --> 00:47:58.811
You want to be non-consensus.

00:47:58.811 --> 00:48:03.689
Cuz if you're right and people are saying
you're violating the laws of physics,

00:48:03.689 --> 00:48:07.450
in the end, you're gonna own
all the intellectual property.

00:48:07.450 --> 00:48:10.870
You're gonna have a tremendous advantage
and, in fact, when you hear of

00:48:10.870 --> 00:48:14.579
the tremendous amount of intellectual
property payments that go to Qualcomm.

00:48:14.579 --> 00:48:21.795
Those were earned by that firm being in
the right non-consensus box early on.

00:48:21.795 --> 00:48:25.315
If everyone already agreed with us,
we'd all be right.

00:48:26.325 --> 00:48:26.875
That's fine.

00:48:26.875 --> 00:48:30.685
It's just not as profitable as being
right in a non-consensus sense.

00:48:32.050 --> 00:48:33.930
But Deena, now look at this.

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:34.560
What if we're wrong?

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:37.830
If we're wrong,
where do you wanna be then?

00:48:38.920 --> 00:48:40.650
And there is no doubt.

00:48:40.650 --> 00:48:47.550
If you're wrong, you just don't wanna be
alone, because nobody wants to be here.

00:48:47.550 --> 00:48:50.044
This is the fool.

00:48:50.044 --> 00:48:55.301
And a big problem we face in trying
to manage innovation is that

00:48:55.301 --> 00:49:01.890
the fear of being a fool is often much
stronger than the hope of being a genius.

00:49:04.459 --> 00:49:09.371
Because of that, odds are people
in your organization have ideas

00:49:09.371 --> 00:49:13.340
that they are afraid to come forward with.

00:49:13.340 --> 00:49:16.380
People will side to consensus thinking,

00:49:16.380 --> 00:49:18.520
even though they might
have brilliant ideas.

00:49:18.520 --> 00:49:23.040
And the question you have to ask yourself
as a leader is that have you created

00:49:23.040 --> 00:49:29.360
an environment in your organization where
it's safe for people to risk being a fool.

00:49:30.390 --> 00:49:31.670
And if the answer to that is no,

00:49:31.670 --> 00:49:36.350
you are not going to hear the ideas
that potentially are genius.

00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:43.890
&gt;&gt; Wow.
Again, can't help thinking about how you,

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:48.500
how you know on the J cur,
but how you know if

00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:52.146
you are actually on the J curve versus
just heading towards a failure.

00:49:52.146 --> 00:49:53.137
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:49:53.137 --> 00:49:56.400
&gt;&gt; And how,
ow you help people who don't like change.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:57.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:49:57.490 --> 00:49:59.840
&gt;&gt; So again, you're heading in this path.

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:03.740
You're trying to help people fail,
and you're trying to find the genius.

00:50:03.740 --> 00:50:08.560
And you're surrounded by people who want
results and who don't, sort of, I guess,

00:50:08.560 --> 00:50:10.720
honor or respect the failure.

00:50:10.720 --> 00:50:12.170
So, how do you help that?

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:13.160
That, the fool.

00:50:13.160 --> 00:50:15.035
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Yeah, you know, it's, it's,

00:50:15.035 --> 00:50:19.094
constantly around the business school
Deena, we hear people saying, you know,

00:50:19.094 --> 00:50:21.720
I've come here to learn about
best business practice.

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:26.419
Or we've come here to understand how to
interpret the evidence we're getting in

00:50:26.419 --> 00:50:27.880
our quarterly results.

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:32.040
We're very inductive from data that way.

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:33.380
We wanna see the results.

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:34.640
How do I, how can I tell?

00:50:34.640 --> 00:50:35.680
How can I model this?

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:36.400
How can I know?

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:39.680
If the idea is really innovative,

00:50:39.680 --> 00:50:44.810
you can't know with respect to
the experience of the firm.

00:50:44.810 --> 00:50:46.190
There is risk.

00:50:46.190 --> 00:50:47.350
That's unavoidable.

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:51.800
What we can do in advance
is elaborate on our logic.

00:50:51.800 --> 00:50:56.050
The Stanford Business School in our
new curriculum has focused greatly

00:50:56.050 --> 00:51:00.410
on helping our students think
logically about strategy.

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:06.050
The reason we did this is that the, the,
what leadership can do is offer an,

00:51:06.050 --> 00:51:09.390
a logic for
where it is the firm wants to go.

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:12.110
Now, that logic can have flaws and

00:51:12.110 --> 00:51:16.000
will need to be updated in
an iterative way as evidence comes in.

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:19.640
But the nature of innovation is that
if the innovation is big enough,

00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:21.950
we don't have the data now to know.

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:26.268
We didn't have the data in 1990
to know how we would look at

00:51:26.268 --> 00:51:28.519
the World Wide Web by 2000.

00:51:28.519 --> 00:51:32.789
We didn't have the data when they were
first making social network sites to

00:51:32.789 --> 00:51:35.363
realize that they would be platforms for

00:51:35.363 --> 00:51:38.770
sharing pictures within ten
years of their founding.

00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:42.088
And we can go through example after
example such as we've done over this,

00:51:42.088 --> 00:51:43.745
the course of this webinar.

00:51:43.745 --> 00:51:48.475
But what we did have,
was the capacity to engage in logic,

00:51:49.915 --> 00:51:51.725
and it is a primary job for

00:51:51.725 --> 00:51:56.610
leadership to help their people
understand the logic of the business.

00:51:56.610 --> 00:52:01.390
The strategy is a retrospective
rationalization of what we've done as

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:04.660
a firm but it is also a roadmap for

00:52:04.660 --> 00:52:08.240
the logic that should get
us through the J-curve.

00:52:08.240 --> 00:52:09.485
Are we going to be right?

00:52:09.485 --> 00:52:12.345
Of course not, in fact,
we need to build failure

00:52:12.345 --> 00:52:16.755
into the system as we discover
our way through the J-curve.

00:52:16.755 --> 00:52:18.605
But without leadership and logic,

00:52:18.605 --> 00:52:24.015
if all we're relying on are short
term results, we're never going

00:52:24.015 --> 00:52:28.249
to take the kind of risk that gets us to
that much higher level of innovation.

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:31.680
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:34.860
You know, I want to just mention that
if we were in one of your programs,

00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:36.180
which I highly recommend

00:52:37.460 --> 00:52:40.570
people would have time to ask you
questions throughout the presentation.

00:52:40.570 --> 00:52:43.580
But also to talk with you on breaks and
at meal times.

00:52:43.580 --> 00:52:46.930
And so, we have a lot of questions that
we're not gonna be able to get to.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:50.170
But, I just wanna make sure
that everybody's aware that,

00:52:50.170 --> 00:52:55.330
that in our programs it, you get to
continue the conversation, both,

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:58.590
with the faculty member and
with the, the cohort.

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:03.481
And so, we don't have a huge amount of
time left and there are a lot of things

00:53:03.481 --> 00:53:08.080
that you've said that I, I wanna make
sure that we repeat and get out.

00:53:08.080 --> 00:53:14.782
And so if you could just come back to
sort of the communicating failures.

00:53:14.782 --> 00:53:15.502
&gt;&gt; Mm, mm-hm.

00:53:15.502 --> 00:53:18.231
&gt;&gt; I mean, I think you've touched on
the strategy which is really wonderful.

00:53:18.231 --> 00:53:21.129
But maybe we could just end with
how you communicate the failures so

00:53:21.129 --> 00:53:24.132
that everybody doesn't,
doesn't make the same mistake again, or

00:53:24.132 --> 00:53:26.532
that maybe someone else can
find that path and move on?

00:53:26.532 --> 00:53:29.818
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you know, it's a,
it's a difficult thing and I brought up

00:53:29.818 --> 00:53:34.260
the example of Qualcomm before and
I'd like to raise it again as an example.

00:53:34.260 --> 00:53:39.177
The chief learning officer at Qualcomm's
a, a woman named Tamar Elkeles and

00:53:39.177 --> 00:53:44.018
has provided, I think, a lot of us with
a tremendous example with how to deal

00:53:44.018 --> 00:53:46.765
with the question that you asked, Deena.

00:53:46.765 --> 00:53:48.441
It's been a very successful company,

00:53:48.441 --> 00:53:51.180
she's been their chief
learning officer for years.

00:53:51.180 --> 00:53:55.842
What's interesting is as a c level
officer right there with top management,

00:53:55.842 --> 00:53:59.226
she's been really pushing
on the innovation process.

00:53:59.226 --> 00:54:03.738
And in particular,
when you come to be a Qualcomm employee,

00:54:03.738 --> 00:54:09.496
like every firm, you go through
an initial process of orientation.

00:54:09.496 --> 00:54:12.990
What's interesting at Qualcomm is that
you will, you will hear films, and

00:54:12.990 --> 00:54:16.330
many of those films are top-level Qualcomm

00:54:17.580 --> 00:54:21.230
leaders talking about
their greatest failures.

00:54:22.430 --> 00:54:28.700
And you know, from the beginning,
they focus on the fact that well-intended

00:54:28.700 --> 00:54:34.050
behavior that leads to failing
outcomes have very good implications.

00:54:34.050 --> 00:54:39.860
Obviously, you don't want behaviors
that are against policy or

00:54:39.860 --> 00:54:42.350
in some way, nefarious.

00:54:42.350 --> 00:54:43.690
That's not what we're getting at.

00:54:43.690 --> 00:54:47.300
But we're talking about people trying to
do their job where there's a inherent

00:54:47.300 --> 00:54:49.990
element of risk and things don't go well.

00:54:49.990 --> 00:54:54.810
If that's a career-ending event, people
throughout an organization know that.

00:54:54.810 --> 00:55:00.341
And they get very conservative, and the,
the great innovative organizations of

00:55:00.341 --> 00:55:06.212
the world try to create a culture where in
some contexts, it's safe to be innovative.

